\description{
Supervised ordination of samples from their feature abundances: the
components of a (normalized) count table that best separate known groups.
PLS-DA is partial least squares (PLS) regression of a dummy matrix of the
groups on the table. Gives the same results as scikit-learn's
\code{PLSRegression} and \code{mixOmics::plsda()}, computed straight from sparse
input without forming any dense table or \eqn{n \times n}{n x n} matrix.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

Let \eqn{X} be the normalized table, its features centred (and, with
\code{scale = TRUE}, scaled to unit variance), and \eqn{Y} the dummy matrix of
the groups (one column per group, 1 for that group's samples and 0 for
the others), centred (and scaled) the same way. PLS regression (Wold et
al. 2001) finds one component at a time. With \eqn{X_a} and \eqn{Y_a}
what is left of \eqn{X} and \eqn{Y} before component \eqn{a}:
\itemize{
\item the weights \eqn{w} are the top left singular vector of
\eqn{X_a^T Y_a}, the direction in which \eqn{X_a} covaries most with
\eqn{Y_a};
\item the scores are \eqn{t = X_a w};
\item the loadings are \eqn{p = X_a^T t / |t|^2} and the Y loadings
\eqn{q = Y_a^T t / |t|^2};
\item both tables are deflated by the scores: \eqn{X_{a+1} = X_a - t p^T}
and \eqn{Y_{a+1} = Y_a - t q^T} (regression mode).
}

Barker & Rayens (2003) give the link to discriminant analysis. Fitting
stops early, with a warning, when \eqn{X_a^T Y_a} falls to
\eqn{\sqrt{\epsilon} \approx 1.5 \times 10^{-8}}{sqrt(eps) = 1.5e-8} times
its value for the first component: then what is left of the table has
no covariance with the groups, beyond rounding error (for instance, once
the components span the whole table).

VIP (Wold et al. 2001; Mehmood et al. 2012) sums each feature's squared
weights over the first \eqn{a} components, each weighted by the
component's share of the explained sum of squares of \eqn{Y},
\eqn{SSY_b = |t_b|^2 |q_b|^2}:
\deqn{VIP_j = \sqrt{P \sum_{b \le a} SSY_b w_{jb}^2 / \sum_{b \le a} SSY_b}}{VIP_j = sqrt(P * sum(SSY_b * w_jb^2) / sum(SSY_b))}
for \eqn{P} features (constant ones included, when \code{scale} leaves them
out). The mean of the squared VIPs is 1, so features with a VIP above 1
are often called important.

The table is never centred in memory: products with \eqn{X_a} are
computed from the non-zero values alone, in parallel, as in \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, so
a sparse table is never made dense.

\strong{Choosing \code{norm}}

For microbiome counts, \code{norm = 'clr'} (with a \code{pseudocount}) is the usual
choice, as in \code{mixOmics}' \code{logratio = 'CLR'}: PLS-DA then works on
log-ratios, which suit compositional data.

\strong{Validate the separation}

PLS-DA is supervised: it looks for the directions that separate the
groups, so its plots separate them even when the groups don't differ
(with more features than samples, almost always). Don't read the plot as
evidence of a difference. Test it with a permutation test of the
distances, such as \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}, or estimate the error rate of
classifying new samples by cross-validation.

\strong{Matching scikit-learn and mixOmics}

\code{plsda()} gives the same scores, weights, loadings and Y loadings as
scikit-learn's \code{PLSRegression(n_components, scale = True)} fitted to the
normalized table and the dummy matrix, up to the signs of the
components, and the same scores, weights (\code{loadings$X}), explained
variance (\code{prop_expl_var$X}) and VIP (\code{mixOmics::vip()}) as
\code{mixOmics::plsda()}. (With \code{scale = FALSE}, \code{mixOmics::vip()} weights each
component by its squared correlations with the dummy columns rather than
by \eqn{SSY}, which is the same only when they are scaled; \code{plsda()}
keeps the textbook formula.) Both fit each component by NIPALS, a power iteration that converges to
the weights above; \code{plsda()} computes them directly instead, as
scikit-learn's \code{algorithm = "svd"} does, so it matches them when they
are run with a small tolerance (\code{tol}). With their default tolerances,
their results with three groups or more can differ from the converged
ones by about 0.1\%. With two groups, NIPALS converges at once.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{mixOmics::plsda()} and \code{caret::plsda()} share their names with
ecodive's \code{plsda()}, and whichever package was attached last masks the
other's. Write \code{ecodive::plsda()}, or use \code{\link[=count_ord]{count_ord()}}, which always runs
ecodive's, to be sure.

\strong{Component signs and reproducibility}

A component's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return mirror
images of the same component. \code{plsda()} flips each component so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips its weights,
loadings and Y loadings with it. Results are identical from run to run
and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last, the records
of how it was made (see \link{ecodive_steps}): \code{dist}, the distance
matrix's, for the methods that take distances, and \code{ord}, the
ordination's own (its method, display name and settings). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation. If
\code{norm = 'clr'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # PLS-DA of iris, as if its measurements were counts.
    counts <- round(iris[, 1:4] * 10)
    ord    <- plsda(counts, 'Species', iris)
    plot(ord)
    attr(ord, 'prop')
    attr(ord, 'vip')

    # CLR-transformed microbiome counts.
    grp <- rep(c('a', 'b'), length.out = nrow(ex_counts))
    ord <- plsda(ex_counts, grp, norm = 'clr', pseudocount = 1)
    attr(ord, 'centroids')

    # Supervised: always validate the separation, e.g. with PERMANOVA.
    permanova(aitchison(ex_counts, pseudocount = 1), grp, permutations = 99)

}
